#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403995 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb878275-dd8f-5279-87bb-823b52440a3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403997} {:answer/id #uuid "b6645595-f628-5b3a-b344-09a000390b50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=36\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403998} {:answer/id #uuid "bf5f57ab-d2f9-5daa-9efa-38536f21e341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403999} {:answer/id #uuid "d6a84421-2599-55d0-8ed6-1320197c2f09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404000} {:answer/id #uuid "51da59a3-0b15-57d9-849f-365c0c494c8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404001}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403997} :answer-field/id #uuid "1486ad18-45b8-51c1-922e-f675b1842550" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403996}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fff9a321-6f13-5737-b6f2-c97dfeb1f014" :question/math-academy-id "q-79286" :question/problem "![](images/85/85f4441b721fa0c059c6783357c01b40de46314cf82ad501f9addc518226169f.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-4,-4),$ $a=4$ and $b=6.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404002 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42d6d789-9a13-55ed-9aa3-a6599922e6d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=25\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404004} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0179db-bc3d-59ac-a7ab-f6e35d77cdda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404005} {:answer/id #uuid "22551caa-d93a-5d26-a64b-1d4d526c0551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404006} {:answer/id #uuid "61661834-8abe-5e9e-95ce-3916c77f8307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404007} {:answer/id #uuid "10b69a37-8e76-5d45-bf03-9dcbeccb702d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404008}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404005} :answer-field/id #uuid "6729ae77-9d58-5d6c-9396-7d1f8b6ef681" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9082f86b-7c0f-5dd7-b074-5c0e7d067128" :question/math-academy-id "q-79287" :question/problem "![](images/07/07ec13485a828c8f5cd99ae2fbc3f7444135ba3c1cdf790762edb01b9726317e.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,2),$ $a=3$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404009 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0a50ab1-4ada-57e2-8605-70a502bab6ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404011} {:answer/id #uuid "32f648c8-3d4d-551c-a0fe-e98c1f6d2eba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404012} {:answer/id #uuid "89052cbe-3422-56ea-9b9a-942801acf80e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404013} {:answer/id #uuid "8b13ccd7-add3-5ba4-bf95-5440c51f18c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404014} {:answer/id #uuid "1c440c18-9297-5a46-a77e-a81bd6a4fec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404015}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404011} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d9041eb-bfd0-524e-8602-d57198b3ea25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404010}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7a6a1e67-ac65-5715-8684-1ea8d5d95e68" :question/math-academy-id "q-79292" :question/problem "![](images/7d/7d45616d089e8f5d078ff48c949b26d0920074ab4b611ec71b491a0e18fd293c.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(3,1)$ with horizontal radius $1$ and vertical radius $2,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(4,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,1),$ $a=1$ and $b=2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405132 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "124556c0-f467-5318-900e-7da585d6554e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405134} {:answer/id #uuid "5a7a1b1e-b535-5117-9c36-e366c14a4377" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405135} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1353fa-aa2b-5093-bcea-f8bdbde39ce0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405136} {:answer/id #uuid "699346b8-439a-5fe0-9c40-fa77c142405e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,-1,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405137} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce9fec0-c766-50ce-8550-9639ade8ba3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,\\,0,\\,-2⟩+t\\,⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405138}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405135} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa0a835b-df77-526f-b20f-c5410f491aab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a7c74ce4-3c4e-5ea4-ab88-89558065f279" :question/math-academy-id "q-36754" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface $$\n\\displaystyle {x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1\n$$ at the point $(0,0,2),$ with parameter $t=0$ at this point." :question/worked-solution "First, note that we can write the equation of our surface as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we set $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1,\n$$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,0,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,0,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+\\frac{2y}{9}\\text{ }j+\\frac{z}{2}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,0,2) & =2(0)\\text{ }i+\\frac{2(0)}{9}\\text{ }j+\\frac{(2)}{2}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, the vector form of the equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(0,0,2)$ and passes through the point $(0,0,2)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,∇f(0,0,2)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406771 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06cfa4e4-4978-5d07-b498-b67163d323ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406760} {:answer/id #uuid "47f0e73b-3fcc-5922-ac4c-5cbbb15dc078" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406762} {:answer/id #uuid "4cd27081-6df8-5fcc-87e8-0b763e12dd24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406763} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd4d236-b060-5654-b754-6a3374d3448b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406766} {:answer/id #uuid "ddaabaf5-f0e8-54a9-9f5a-d6c4751feaf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406768}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406760} :answer-field/id #uuid "06a97003-66fd-5b44-944b-7b4ac3d8364b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "84951e54-c705-5b01-85eb-275b777e397c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161810" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}u+vw \\\\ {v}^{2} \\\\ w+v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u+vw,\\,y(u,v,w)={v}^{2},\\,z(u,v,w)=w+v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & w & v \\\\ 0 & 2v & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}2v & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(2v-0) \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2v & =0 \\\\ v & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v=0$ defines the plane $v=0,$ i.e., $uw$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406772 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03b82058-2ed1-5ab3-8be0-f2ccd9e791be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2uv-2u{v}^{2}-vu" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406759} {:answer/id #uuid "2b07851c-5666-5e08-93b4-ea52710578c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{u}^{2}-2{v}^{3}-vw" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406761} {:answer/id #uuid "4e04d287-7eaa-501d-ba5e-706a25e1adcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2vw" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406764} {:answer/id #uuid "78a6574d-a7a7-5dd3-9372-265b4fd8dfa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2uv-2u{v}^{2}+vu" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406765} {:answer/id #uuid "c5d7dba0-5d86-5a7b-97ed-55ed0cf1cb1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{u}^{2}-2{v}^{3}+vw" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406767}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406761} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc7e9e1f-8201-5457-9ae8-866cb0a50e5b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9eac1497-79c1-5ed3-be68-00cfa0edfef5" :question/math-academy-id "q-39710" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}+vw,\\,y=2-uv,\\,z={v}^{2}-w.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & w & v \\\\ -v & -u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}-u & 0 \\\\ 2v & -1\\end{vmatrix}+(-v)(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}w & v \\\\ 2v & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u(u-0)+v(-w-2{v}^{2}) \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}-2{v}^{3}-vw.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406786 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dd5310c-dfc2-5a7d-bac5-5825f5064da6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406775} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd3692c-18d7-5dde-9e79-ca2c71d664a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406776} {:answer/id #uuid "cd07008c-2d5e-5372-aa7f-53d2714271f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5\\,i+2\\,j-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406778} {:answer/id #uuid "e415ebda-6949-5999-a629-48685dbda1c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\,i-5\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406781} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a7f1a1-3119-55e9-ab80-51ececd277a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i-5\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406782}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406776} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f78fc66-8357-5b97-ab1a-5d79ee0e7c85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "077d2dca-c9d8-5043-92cb-18878e5ddc66" :question/math-academy-id "q-71397" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(-2,-3,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy)\\,k \\\\  & =(4x-y)\\text{ }i-x\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(-2,-3,1) & =(4(-2)-(-3))\\,i-(-2)\\,j+2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =-5\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-5\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406787 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a1d5e01-e81c-5544-a929-055747dc7f77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406774} {:answer/id #uuid "82778c6a-7758-50ec-ad08-3720a9d490ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-y-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406777} {:answer/id #uuid "cdae5c41-27fa-5c55-9f46-cceeabd59a1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+y-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406779} {:answer/id #uuid "dbcc8cce-7237-5eb6-ac41-6a5901ea8c81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+2y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406780} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf6508f-66f3-52c3-8fbf-477ac88383db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406783}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406779} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9e7ba4e-7d61-5628-8ce5-894dfba410f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ef115c0b-3981-5c8f-8e9d-f41c20799c2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-130719" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2y-x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2y}\\right).$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2y}\\right) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2y}\\right)-2y+x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2y}\\right)-2y+x,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2y}\\right)-2y+x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2y}\\right)-2y+x)j \\\\  & =(\\frac{2y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2y}+1)i+(\\frac{2y}{x}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{x}{2{y}^{2}}\\right)-2)j \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x}+1)i+(-\\frac{1}{y}-2)j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =(\\frac{1}{2}+1)i+(-\\frac{1}{1}-2)j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-3\\,j \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2},-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(3,1)=⟨\\frac{3}{2},-3⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)} & =\\frac{y-1}{(-3)} \\\\ -3(x-2) & =\\frac{3}{2}(y-1) \\\\ -6(x-2) & =3(y-1) \\\\ -2(x-2) & =y-1 \\\\ -2x+4 & =y-1 \\\\ 2x+y-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407566 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "758d7c9c-7190-58ba-88ca-6ba4209fb9ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/02/02e2b36b593aef6b8477a6e3a546a7a367cd6c7c8de4642684f418222e0d1c96.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407560} {:answer/id #uuid "b0547ab6-4eb4-5605-bedc-e8c09b74d5ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8f/8f79cfa6b73725479bd76db48b212a60b04ed91f3355c9d135e8cf176b3a6b8f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407561} {:answer/id #uuid "b6af969e-293e-5aa6-8efd-61f10b87dbac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/42/426f761b56bbba7549a5d76ecc6340461c3bc76627c9664a46ef4c6063ee7039.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407562} {:answer/id #uuid "e719fb82-e005-592a-9a1d-c08ce892673d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/5f/5f4468465de36289d11b86acb35c38e6d2034ca220d6559ec93b5085d7437d5c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407563} {:answer/id #uuid "eeddcc87-ac3b-50d2-be15-6abdd930a045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/5a/5a05d0be8d775cfa97ae507da2ce7904a11b0f7c8df14d51f2e95f17925f64f7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407564}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407564} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cef7ae1-6ba6-5fd3-82d2-e1546e8de597" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8917ffe4-7713-5b06-82b5-c913e5f2e407" :question/math-academy-id "q-122880" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=6x+2y?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=-1,$ we get $6x+2y=-1,$ which gives the line $y=-3x-\\frac{1}{2}$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get $6x+2y=0,$ which gives the line $y=-3x$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=2,$ we get $6x+2y=2,$ which gives the line $y=-3x+1$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/d6/d6c45af410df1ae974062b777b856b3960e72ad13e14d37a48e5a92cebf74cd1.png)\n\n \n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as both $x$ and  $y$ increase. \n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/3a/3aa601a2bb31bb8c009f554361a79135daf1d7abd2779eaf1ae8b2431a01eafa.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](images/5a/5a05d0be8d775cfa97ae507da2ce7904a11b0f7c8df14d51f2e95f17925f64f7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407846 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "194293e4-0f48-56b7-8094-db759002b936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407825} {:answer/id #uuid "638f4f88-2af6-5eb2-9ed5-e5ceca460c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407831} {:answer/id #uuid "88efc86f-081f-55bf-ae7a-919620a60363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407834} {:answer/id #uuid "ab3f8034-9dc3-50c6-bd5e-b2ea032262ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407835} {:answer/id #uuid "ada75cf0-6667-5096-ac74-83cbe5d47353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407836}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407834} :answer-field/id #uuid "926f9639-4822-59a3-b1ce-00790077ea5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "113d22b7-0222-5499-8d44-de1af29900a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-129340" :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{3}-xy}{{x}^{3}y}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{3}-xy}{{x}^{3}y} & =\\frac{{0}^{3}-2(0)}{{2}^{3}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{3}-xy}{{x}^{3}y} & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{y({y}^{2}-x)}{{x}^{3}y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{\\cancel{y}({y}^{2}-x)}{{x}^{3}\\cancel{y}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,0)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}-2}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407847 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08c4d527-5e01-5264-8001-c16ba5c829f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453880} {:answer/id #uuid "8a788be2-6daa-5a9a-b61f-b090c8b8cf80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453881}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453881} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d46ebdc-4ea8-5b5d-a791-12ebf82b2b2b" :answer-field/key "The limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{3}$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454690} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7662fdd-d050-5004-a857-2d86c30b4a05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453882} {:answer/id #uuid "9233290a-d139-5d2d-91b5-3115178cf2c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453883}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453882} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e9a1015-1583-5e39-b729-2685f362e667" :answer-field/key "The limit evaluates to $-1$ along the path $y=-{x}^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454691} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25f5a5f0-e3d5-58cb-a9a2-e0e244128177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453884} {:answer/id #uuid "6013b3d6-d8f8-5f12-8351-83ccb30ce208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453885}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453884} :answer-field/id #uuid "81040326-5134-5a42-8539-aac88ecd9a5e" :answer-field/key "The limit exists and is equal to $\\frac{1}{3}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a9a907de-0404-5ffb-8362-e9676298070b" :question/math-academy-id "q-36139" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}=\\frac{0}{{0}^{2}+2\\cdot 0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{3}$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$” is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $-1$ along the path $y=-{x}^{2}$” is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-{x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-{x}^{2}}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2(-{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit exists and is equal to $\\frac{1}{3}$” is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+2y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1408b7a6-0c53-5b8e-9133-67f17a92be59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407824} {:answer/id #uuid "195220e5-a0bf-53a4-9715-d520b9cc06cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407826} {:answer/id #uuid "4272af7f-33bd-5340-9cab-dc535b5219ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407829} {:answer/id #uuid "6e9919cf-e851-589f-8631-eb084d75d2b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407832} {:answer/id #uuid "af13b1b6-a010-5d4f-9f5a-9200139b2880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407837}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407829} :answer-field/id #uuid "356ed38c-7b44-5965-81ac-2c88dd16e0ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d70f2b2e-153e-52a8-b763-677074637b88" :question/math-academy-id "q-74955" :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{xy+2}-2}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{xy+2}-2}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 1+2}-2}{{2}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}-4}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{xy+2}-2}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4} & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{xy+2}-2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)}{({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{xy+2})^{2}-4}{({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{xy-2}{(xy-2)(xy+2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{1}{(xy+2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{1}{(xy+2)(\\sqrt{xy+2}+2)} & =\\frac{1}{(2\\cdot 1+2)(\\sqrt{2\\cdot 1+2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{16}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407849 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39f90210-da8d-511b-9d38-b3d158c315d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407827} {:answer/id #uuid "4005330f-5c8b-5294-9f96-5e9be2626147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407828} {:answer/id #uuid "88b04b8f-a6d9-5b86-ba06-a0eb6a004245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407833} {:answer/id #uuid "b72aa7bf-e6dc-52f1-8358-bfb499369454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407838} {:answer/id #uuid "d17e2afc-964e-5d9b-b663-5d2974b50bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407839}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407828} :answer-field/id #uuid "e093674e-caf5-5f8d-97ff-ca3172fa6889" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fac05de1-cec1-5741-a5ec-beb8567ada24" :question/math-academy-id "q-129343" :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{y+z}{\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z}}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{y+z}{\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z}} & =\\frac{1+(-1)}{\\sqrt{2-1}-\\sqrt{2+(-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{y+z}{\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z}} & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{(y+z)(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{(\\sqrt{x-y}-\\sqrt{x+z})(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{(y+z)(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{(x-y)-(x+z)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{(y+z)(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{-(y+z)} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}\\frac{\\cancel{(y+z)}(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})}{\\cancel{(y+z)}} \\\\  & =-\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\lim_{x\\to (2,1,-1)}(\\sqrt{x-y}+\\sqrt{x+z}) & =-(\\sqrt{2-1}+\\sqrt{2+(-1)}) \\\\  & =-(1+1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411859 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18b329fb-ed63-5c9c-ba4f-1ae88547c789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411696} {:answer/id #uuid "3565a038-62ce-5c59-8e95-c3505e80c675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411705} {:answer/id #uuid "46436b71-8e85-5a39-bf27-5ed0f753e896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411714} {:answer/id #uuid "c32ac31a-d68d-5bbe-8d37-51261f4658fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411784} {:answer/id #uuid "fbdd4223-88ec-5c0a-a0b3-1dc095b07709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411816}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411816} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ed96f7f-49a8-554d-a91d-a5525992b220" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "58242fe6-4f94-5872-a2af-27a25e52256a" :question/math-academy-id "q-124278" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d83f771aa650879909c33d7033e80ca044b7e6f8369e19667e6173aa8a40737.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(x+\\sqrt{3y})\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,3),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(2,0)$ and $(0,3).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(2+t(0-2)),\\,(0+t(3-0))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-2t,\\,3t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{3y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2-2t)+\\sqrt{3(3t)} \\\\  & =2-2t+3\\sqrt{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-2t+3\\sqrt{t})\\cdot 3\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-2t+3{t}^{1/2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{[2t-{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3[(2(1)-(1)^{2}+2(1)^{3/2})-(0)] \\\\  & =3(3) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411866 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ea71a81-55b2-5821-a85d-2c2b860ef761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411729} {:answer/id #uuid "68da978e-5392-5c13-80d0-e709f72c6da6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411734} {:answer/id #uuid "6e0953bd-cd3e-5daf-9baa-978871a2e773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411737} {:answer/id #uuid "98fb62ce-1156-5b60-96a5-36d7a22956c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411759} {:answer/id #uuid "a31dfc4c-cfac-5c12-8881-aa0d90cb5c66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411764}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411734} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e959dbb-ec9c-5316-8bef-b8459b6fb499" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411827}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8fec53f8-0962-53b9-8a63-104c832ed68f" :question/math-academy-id "q-160710" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v=x+y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the lines $x+y=1,$ $x-y=1$ and the curve ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x-y,\\,v=x+y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x+y=v$ into the equation of the first line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y=1\\,⟹\\,v=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x-y=u$ into the equation of the second line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y=1\\,⟹\\,u=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x+y=v$ and $x-y=u$ into the equation of curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=2\\,⟹\\,(x-y)(x+y)=2,\\,⟹\\,uv=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection point of the two lines in the $uv$-plane is $(1,1).$\n\nTo find the intersection of the curve with the first line in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}uv &=2 \\\\ v &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=2 \\\\ v &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the intersections of the curve with the second line in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}uv &=2 \\\\ u &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=1 \\\\ v &=2.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the intersection points are $(1,1),$ $(1,2)$ and $(2,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le \\frac{2}{u}\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/4c/4c151e7fc8d150b9c8def69d609446b77424c2703decd9c8c55866cff2ae7437.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(2)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{2/u}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}[v]_{1}^{2/u}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{2}{u}-1)du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡u-u]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-2+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414460 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31c23b1e-7cd7-5e7a-8c99-254c33c9229b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414434} {:answer/id #uuid "b146a4db-c677-57ea-92f8-07f41239596d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}{u}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414445} {:answer/id #uuid "b723dafe-f5a6-5a70-be14-953187b88dde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{u}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414446} {:answer/id #uuid "c87217ff-e3d3-5223-ac71-463a0c197918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{u}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414448} {:answer/id #uuid "e4f2c71c-8604-5aa1-9ce7-1663a5f2e6db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{u}^{2}+{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414453}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414445} :answer-field/id #uuid "16fadbea-4516-5df4-9f64-8b7cc353d5a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "26a444d6-a870-593f-bb03-1c02941c48b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-161124" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,u,\\,v⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+0} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,{|}_{x=u\\,y=u\\,z=v} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}+{u}^{2} \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2\\sqrt{2}{u}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414461 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cd71aa2-2da0-53d3-a18e-57cc7dd66fe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{u}^{2}(-{u}^{3}+{v}^{4})\\sqrt{4uv+u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414430} {:answer/id #uuid "6a78c70d-c252-5fa1-b3b9-b5253f7112ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{u}^{2}(-{u}^{3}-{v}^{4})\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414437} {:answer/id #uuid "a93dd97e-74c6-56c8-a6b1-3cdadc7213e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{u}^{2}({u}^{4}-{v}^{3})\\sqrt{4{u}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414443} {:answer/id #uuid "cb42e303-0573-52db-81ce-d57e99e6f761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{v}^{2}({u}^{2}+{v}^{4})\\sqrt{{v}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414449} {:answer/id #uuid "d505a7da-72d5-5c19-aa46-75ea67cf0bb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u(-{u}^{3}-{v}^{4})\\sqrt{4{v}^{3}+u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414451}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414437} :answer-field/id #uuid "72a815fc-9d19-53b2-b0ae-b65abcc40317" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2c7beff7-ff72-566b-ae77-d272cca30605" :question/math-academy-id "q-161288" :question/problem "$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nConsider the surface integral of the function \n$f(x,y,z)=-x-{z}^{2}$ \nover the parametric surface \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨{u}^{3},\\,v,\\,{v}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D⊆{R}^{2}.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{u}^{2},\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3{u}^{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}3{u}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}3{u}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-6v{u}^{2},\\,3{u}^{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖ & =‖⟨0,\\,-6v{u}^{2},\\,3{u}^{2}⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+(-6v{u}^{2})^{2}+(3{u}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{v}^{2}{u}^{4}+9{u}^{4}} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(-x-{z}^{2})\\,{|}_{x={u}^{3}\\,y=v\\,z={v}^{2}} \\\\  & =-{u}^{3}-({v}^{2}\\cdot {v}^{2}) \\\\  & =-{u}^{3}-{v}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,3{u}^{2}(-{u}^{3}-{v}^{4})\\sqrt{4{v}^{2}+1}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22129875-cc14-5ad3-8bba-b26995c05188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414433} {:answer/id #uuid "38973f90-0f85-5e71-8380-79a67500ceed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414435} {:answer/id #uuid "44291a57-8b30-555c-b7e9-95314ffa4f9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414436} {:answer/id #uuid "ab922d47-0766-574c-83bb-4e4ccfbb77ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414444} {:answer/id #uuid "e1fe1620-65f9-5859-8a82-9a5f97b2fafb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414452}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414452} :answer-field/id #uuid "93abc2ba-af03-5472-9cce-10fe847607a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d27e0ee0-ad93-5416-8abb-f21167765a8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161181" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(3x-2y+3z)\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2u+v,\\,2u+v,\\,-v⟩\n$$\n\n \nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,-1\\le v\\le 0\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=2\\sqrt{2}.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(3x-2y+3z)\\,{|}_{x=2u+v\\,y=2u+v\\,z=-v} \\\\  & =3(2u+v)-2(2u+v)+3(-v) \\\\  & =2(u-v)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2(u-v)\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,(u-v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}({\\int }_{-1}^{1}u-v\\,\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}-vu]}_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(0-2v)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{[-{v}^{2}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414463 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b5899eb-77cf-5b34-b910-c93474d97e73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414432} {:answer/id #uuid "6d9b5bd3-6406-550a-9325-8fcec12f33ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414438} {:answer/id #uuid "8846a5c5-d563-5056-8e15-64f5e19db2bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414441} {:answer/id #uuid "8d5f84da-ca2b-5738-b498-90f08c5871a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414442} {:answer/id #uuid "f2e130f7-33b8-53a2-a94a-0675fb611102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414454}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414438} :answer-field/id #uuid "831c917a-be0b-5705-bc09-e4bee9b35277" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f95319f6-2e43-511b-ae0d-9608a991c217" :question/math-academy-id "q-161179" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(x+y+z)\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by \n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\,3v,\\,2u-v⟩\n$$\n\n \nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that $‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=3\\sqrt{6}.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice that we are told that the norm of the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖=3\\sqrt{6}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(u,v)) & =(x+y+z)\\,{|}_{x=u+v\\,y=3v\\,z=2u-v} \\\\  & =(u+v)+(3v)+(2u-v) \\\\  & =3u+3v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(r(u,v))‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(3u+3v)\\cdot 3\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,(u+v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({\\int }_{0}^{1}u+v\\,\\text{d}u)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+vu]}_{0}^{1}\\text{d}v \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{2}+v)\\text{d}v \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{6}{[\\frac{v}{2}+\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{6}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414783 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "740bd403-f1e6-5a92-a2b9-efa1ee0f552f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472742} {:answer/id #uuid "5170cfef-7833-5d81-bf15-5bd1c2583c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472743}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472742} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ab5fa05-0008-5864-bfbd-37a853fa3a21" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473766} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13931620-6744-5279-819c-a867d3d2f624" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472744} {:answer/id #uuid "89f7ef7d-3d29-5bcd-86e4-6ccb6a042395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472745}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472745} :answer-field/id #uuid "0562aeab-6992-5c67-82d7-eee722fd8045" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473767} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "194dcf31-6ded-5f7d-8ad4-8503fc14610b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472746} {:answer/id #uuid "cff66d04-5359-528e-90cc-f15b10b85371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472747}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472746} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cfac123-c198-5997-bc78-bc4c79e3be46" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0b65bfe0-6178-564e-825d-19d4ac9443d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-150012" :question/problem "![](images/5a/5a3961346a4bcb0d94e46caf6d6ed1253dc56fb4761db07b11829e329bc2fa8d.png)\n\nConsider the part of parabola and constant vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the part of parabola is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ is true. As the particle moves from $A$ to $B,$ the force $F$ works against the particle for $x>0,$ while $F$  works with the particle for $x<0.$ \n\n![](images/af/af80eab6427f376084301c03715fd098cfe4a1c816dcd72d3e1129cfa35ff29d.png)\n\n \n\nBy symmetry, the contribution to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for $x<0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $x>0.$ So,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/a2/a26ca79400bf30aa891543d30a052a6e8ec071ccb48fcd8c418fe0a713dad212.png)\n\n \n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414784 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f0162ba-5ef3-5ddf-8884-68e373c833a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472526} {:answer/id #uuid "10f070d6-ca76-58f9-8198-433b788e83cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472527}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472527} :answer-field/id #uuid "a22ff3ac-6762-5a5a-a846-c9c1ac079ce7" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473658} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a55ad75-30aa-5338-9705-0e8bb5a17796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472528} {:answer/id #uuid "2bfb901c-eb55-5a16-8c43-2a7bc4df1694" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472529}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472528} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e84a8a1-9e86-5904-8f0e-d9bd26a8cf86" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473659} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf9bee40-6700-53ec-bc0a-8c4fbe9bdd4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472530} {:answer/id #uuid "35fcedff-2ccf-5690-9d0e-4989e5420282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472531}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472530} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a2b2811-e1ba-5e92-bdc1-f1a06a417ca6" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "277ca4a2-1c02-58e4-b62f-d0e1176e96df" :question/math-academy-id "q-125499" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8ffc97d4fc442503eb5b59e01f317cc7a77cf57d041e16807aa0645f962a5aeb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the three-quarter circle is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/c9/c9805268d9f4984983c3cc5e2200a0c22d804bc67076a3505ad35471693c037b.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/da/dafb4d468e7711a804a5141301a02331bb5c7900f018a8ecdb6fd382bc05b799.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414785 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00c75fe3-0f58-55e1-8f86-a88338d5cfcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472520} {:answer/id #uuid "a60b9432-a58a-5243-9f31-007a718f9157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472521}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472521} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ce59e55-a6c7-5acf-b0ee-3ead7fb9939a" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473655} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f1a6c81-b372-55cc-97ce-f7cde5c58ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472522} {:answer/id #uuid "8cd28fc9-2509-520a-9b6e-b710a4fcd873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472523}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472522} :answer-field/id #uuid "69033084-6739-5ed3-8082-9c13508586c2" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473656} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f261d4a8-0a18-578f-91e8-66fc2b9fc81b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472524} {:answer/id #uuid "f8017c78-c722-5466-81ee-36047fd63b5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472525}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472524} :answer-field/id #uuid "149a514a-7fb2-5fae-8638-fa7be7c75462" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "429525c1-21c9-588e-9357-69745fab8529" :question/math-academy-id "q-125488" :question/problem "![](images/f7/f712612e5cf628e2cf6f2aac5195bf98b620edee4cab1791829a4ade13bcee15.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/87/8726ab8c3d764016e1bfa5206769674b6809ab3ea047b03734ae5141f0b553b9.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/56/56e77c37324ca2f28c0a09d762d1e5e1f4a29f5f00695e5cbadca748d92b1c6f.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414786 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "862b5ba7-c160-5733-9f50-a71ae76ca34b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472544} {:answer/id #uuid "e41ef8d6-645f-5335-a725-5df3f79d720b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472545}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472544} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6758123-2b6f-5ca7-b758-0977ff127c63" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473667} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87432ad0-fee2-5a6d-93c2-e0fdd6855b6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472546} {:answer/id #uuid "a81d96e7-72a4-5890-9d53-4c56d8d05129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472547}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472547} :answer-field/id #uuid "7218a528-433c-5908-a059-6caac226974f" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473668} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e9d4872-a5a3-5eb5-a69c-5fd7c7c550ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472548} {:answer/id #uuid "90531cfb-e714-59c4-8c1c-8472694477e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472549}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472549} :answer-field/id #uuid "2459159f-cb74-5c50-a417-695621954be1" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7554a362-8a42-5a06-a982-68a08488fe59" :question/math-academy-id "q-125687" :question/problem "![](images/1b/1ba55883d76b7e48148febcf6203a987575e78179c2fcde1963ab9db34a37738.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the сurve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$  works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/e7/e783fb958f01da9552ef4258adf4bed1f7b083520ad9a59082b5479dc2fc7d40.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/20/20798c6992f536d4f280243746f8599bd4589bd69e253caeed65150b8a9944b7.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415103 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10c8a508-1d4a-574f-bb45-6efb1ced71fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415068} {:answer/id #uuid "47d59bce-11d1-59ca-a980-8662920cfd55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415076} {:answer/id #uuid "cdbbdd8b-85ea-5741-9623-bb79a02c5a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415090} {:answer/id #uuid "d992fc1d-6425-5951-9f5f-b0d131ab532a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415094} {:answer/id #uuid "e72ad3e5-d9a9-5e06-b65e-7fe892762e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{e}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415096}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415090} :answer-field/id #uuid "5aa64add-60f3-564a-9626-a2687efca2f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "03426578-7377-543b-bd88-93276fa901ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-104638" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+1,{t}^{2}-1,{t}^{2}-2t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,-1,0⟩ \\\\ r(2) & =⟨5,3,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,-1,0)$ and $(5,3,0).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(5,3,0)-f(1,-1,0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {e}^{5(0)}-(-1)\\cdot {e}^{1(0)} \\\\  & =3+1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415104 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12811091-da24-5f9e-a3cc-d521c187f782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415069} {:answer/id #uuid "48539c5b-fd63-583d-8da2-566a4dd6e0bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415077} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d2cb1e-5c01-5454-b9f6-ddf29ce394b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415082} {:answer/id #uuid "b864bbf5-46ca-5a26-aa58-751cd0107a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415087} {:answer/id #uuid "cdf966f8-fa1b-5eaf-ab84-b21f9b032f94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415091}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415091} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab184202-13b3-5378-a512-5889441d45af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6e8a51b9-ad21-56cd-a267-db39e81294ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-104624" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y+{x}^{2}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,{t}^{2}-1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,-1⟩ \\\\ r(2) & =⟨4,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-1)$ and $(4,3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(4,3)-f(0,-1) \\\\  & =(3+{4}^{2})-(-1+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =(3+16)-(-1) \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415105 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29a72800-4e23-513e-92c2-5437578df2cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}+\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415072} {:answer/id #uuid "62d13908-df4e-538d-9943-7c76652cbac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415079} {:answer/id #uuid "aad335b1-b4de-568f-b2e2-0f2fa5a05048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415084} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a24286-6aba-57bd-af78-4a37875e9372" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415085} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ede2a6-76f1-5387-b6b8-e8fb84166eba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415088}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415072} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c9c0c78-b891-5f32-8dca-852634763b9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9399f137-a4f5-51b7-a84e-790d740d6a70" :question/math-academy-id "q-104732" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t,\\frac{\\pi t}{2}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for $F=(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y)\\,i-({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-x)\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) & =⟨1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+1\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))-({0}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0+0\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}+\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415106 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ceeb53b-391b-5fef-9ab5-1097ef938825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415073} {:answer/id #uuid "2f083f8e-a1a4-56c3-aea6-e98468b6122b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415074} {:answer/id #uuid "b6b875c4-e194-5a11-823c-e0a27f17aa6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415086} {:answer/id #uuid "cc5489e8-7985-5f2a-b47e-2c5f4e8a4b10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415089} {:answer/id #uuid "d782c18b-724f-503c-9bd1-3a2cfd6e6827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415093}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415074} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6b783c0-b546-54f4-ac0c-0b114a515648" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a59364c1-7975-54ce-83e7-b32f8ae82486" :question/math-academy-id "q-104639" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},{t}^{4},{t}^{6}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,1,1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,1,1)-f(0,0,0) \\\\  & =(1\\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{3})-(0\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot {0}^{3}) \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415107 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e6f935a-7227-56de-9d74-d58f3261b90a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415071} {:answer/id #uuid "376dde01-d46a-5414-9e84-a7d099ab2f33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415075} {:answer/id #uuid "75de655b-bac7-5de2-9e4c-ce56729e4a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi {e}^{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415080} {:answer/id #uuid "87f317ce-1ebd-53fe-bd1c-2786b6ebd37d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415081} {:answer/id #uuid "db14b874-47ec-5b46-a18d-4f53face6c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415095}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415075} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fdf3bea-7332-5701-aa59-1894b6d9cc82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f3d23b94-95d1-5af0-9020-b1e5d5ab8286" :question/math-academy-id "q-104637" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y{x}^{2}{e}^{xy}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =⟨0,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0)$ and $(0,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f((r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(0,2)-f(1,0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (0)^{2}\\cdot {e}^{0(2)}-0\\cdot (1)^{2}\\cdot {e}^{1(0)} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f548fd7-8b35-5ec3-9cec-fbc79515f307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415406} {:answer/id #uuid "68411306-3576-579b-8a21-34c1ab7c2b84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415416} {:answer/id #uuid "910075a4-9923-557c-b9e3-c1e2ff793ed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415422} {:answer/id #uuid "d7d88b10-d2ad-5c06-9db7-9b7365276f6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415426} {:answer/id #uuid "f7735ce7-7b0f-5b5a-82f6-7e753a067e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415430}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415406} :answer-field/id #uuid "0df18aab-7e84-55d8-803f-e0704648d1b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "228970be-ca08-547c-9937-b7e76a0b4145" :question/math-academy-id "q-81186" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(-2,0)$ to the point $B(0,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the parametrization of the curve $C.$ Since it's a circle if radius $2$ centered at the origin, the curve $C$ can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve we have that $x(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}y\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(y\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(-4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t{|}_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )] \\\\  & =2(0-0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415440 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "256764ed-4369-50dc-930e-662876f022a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415404} {:answer/id #uuid "4130f50c-9761-535e-832c-969370eba9c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415410} {:answer/id #uuid "56de169b-e6cc-53f1-9778-d57c45a41155" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415413} {:answer/id #uuid "72db92dd-d3bb-5244-9ae0-06d36401d8d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415417} {:answer/id #uuid "fb33e709-cf30-5cd2-9a8f-17fc862403a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415431}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415417} :answer-field/id #uuid "b64329ed-5836-53c3-881a-5ef3bf67996e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "26043436-d017-5e07-ac9c-a4df40dc0429" :question/math-academy-id "q-90895" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}y\\,dx-x\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(\\sqrt{2},0)$ to the point $B(0,\\sqrt{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the parametrization of the curve $C.$ Since it's a circle if radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin, the curve $C$ can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve we have that $x(t)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y(t)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx-x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}y\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(y\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}-x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(-2{sin}^{2}⁡t-2{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2t{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-2[\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0] \\\\  & =-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
